When declaring multiple body parameters in a FastAPI endpoint, the request body needs to be structured as a JSON object where each body parameter is represented as a key. For example, if you have two Pydantic models, `Item` and `User`, the body should look like this:

```JSON
{
    "item": {
        "name": "Foo",
        "description": "The pretender",
        "price": 42.0,
        "tax": 3.2
    },
    "user": {
        "username": "dave",
        "full_name": "Dave Grohl"
    }
}
```

Here, `item` and `user` are the keys corresponding to the parameters defined in your FastAPI path operation function ([https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/body-multiple-params/]). 

In your FastAPI function, it could look something like this:

```python
@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
async def update_item(item_id: int, item: Item, user: User):
    # Logic here
```

This means FastAPI will expect the body to contain these keys, effectively allowing you to manage multiple body parameters efficiently ([https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/body-multiple-params/]).

